############## Install Git, Create a GitHub Account and Execute Various Git Operations ##################

STEP 1: INSTALL GIT

Download Git from:
https://git-scm.com/downloads

Install Git using the default options.

After installation, open Command Prompt / PowerShell / Git Bash and verify:

git --version


STEP 2: CONFIGURE GIT

Set the username:

git config --global user.name "Your Name"

Set the email:

git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"

Check the configuration:

git config --list


STEP 3: CREATE A GITHUB ACCOUNT

1. Open https://github.com
2. Click Sign Up.
3. Enter your email address.
4. Create a password.
5. Choose a GitHub username.
6. Verify your email address.
7. Complete the account setup.


STEP 4: CREATE A NEW REPOSITORY ON GITHUB

1. Login to GitHub.
2. Click the "+" button.
3. Select "New repository".
4. Enter repository name:

git-demo

5. Select Public.
6. Click "Create repository".


STEP 5: CREATE A LOCAL GIT REPOSITORY

Create a project directory:

mkdir git-demo
cd git-demo

Initialize Git:

git init

Check the repository status:

git status


STEP 6: CREATE A FILE

Create a file named index.html.

Example content:

<html>
<head>
<title>Git Demo</title>
</head>
<body>
<h1>Hello GitHub</h1>
</body>
</html>


STEP 7: CHECK THE STATUS

git status


STEP 8: ADD FILE TO STAGING AREA

Add a specific file:

git add index.html

Or add all files:

git add .


STEP 9: COMMIT THE CHANGES

git commit -m "Initial commit"


STEP 10: VIEW COMMIT HISTORY

git log

For a short history:

git log --oneline


STEP 11: CONNECT LOCAL REPOSITORY TO GITHUB

Copy the repository URL from GitHub.

Example:

git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/git-demo.git

Check the remote:

git remote -v


STEP 12: RENAME THE DEFAULT BRANCH TO MAIN

git branch -M main


STEP 13: PUSH FILES TO GITHUB

git push -u origin main

If GitHub asks for authentication, complete the authentication process.


STEP 14: VERIFY THE REPOSITORY

Open the GitHub repository in the browser.

The index.html file and commit should be visible in the repository.


STEP 15: MODIFY THE FILE

Edit index.html and change:

<h1>Hello GitHub</h1>

to:

<h1>Hello GitHub - Version 2</h1>


STEP 16: CHECK MODIFIED FILE

git status


STEP 17: VIEW CHANGES

git diff


STEP 18: ADD THE MODIFIED FILE

git add index.html


STEP 19: COMMIT THE MODIFICATION

git commit -m "Updated index.html"


STEP 20: PUSH THE NEW COMMIT

git push origin main


STEP 21: CREATE A NEW BRANCH

git branch feature

Check branches:

git branch


STEP 22: SWITCH TO THE NEW BRANCH

git switch feature

Or:

git checkout feature


STEP 23: CREATE A FILE IN THE NEW BRANCH

Create a file named feature.txt.

Add some content to it.


STEP 24: ADD AND COMMIT THE NEW FILE

git add feature.txt

git commit -m "Added feature file"


STEP 25: PUSH THE NEW BRANCH TO GITHUB

git push -u origin feature


STEP 26: SWITCH BACK TO MAIN BRANCH

git switch main


STEP 27: MERGE THE FEATURE BRANCH

git merge feature


STEP 28: PUSH THE MERGED CHANGES

git push origin main


STEP 29: CLONE A GITHUB REPOSITORY

Move to another directory:

cd ..

Clone the repository:

git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/git-demo.git

Enter the cloned repository:

cd git-demo


STEP 30: CHECK REMOTE REPOSITORY

git remote -v


STEP 31: FETCH CHANGES

git fetch


STEP 32: PULL CHANGES

git pull origin main


STEP 33: VIEW BRANCHES

git branch

View local and remote branches:

git branch -a


STEP 34: DELETE A LOCAL BRANCH

git branch -d feature


STEP 35: DELETE A REMOTE BRANCH

git push origin --delete feature


STEP 36: UNSTAGE A FILE

If a file is added using git add:

git restore --staged filename


STEP 37: DISCARD LOCAL CHANGES

git restore filename


STEP 38: VIEW COMMIT DETAILS

git show


STEP 39: VIEW DIFFERENCES BETWEEN COMMITS

git diff HEAD~1 HEAD


STEP 40: CHECK CURRENT GIT STATUS

git status


STEP 41: CHECK CURRENT BRANCH

git branch --show-current


STEP 42: VIEW ALL GIT CONFIGURATION

git config --list


STEP 43: REMOVE GIT REPOSITORY

To remove Git tracking from the project, delete the .git directory.

Windows Command Prompt:

rmdir /s /q .git

Git Bash:

rm -rf .git



